     "<div>On Thursday 4 June 2020 20:47, Wei Guo &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">guowe...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:<br></div><div><br></div><blockquote type=\"cite\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Hi, Vincent,<br></div><div>Thank you so much for your kind reply. Yes, I can find the variable definition. I just made a link table for certain variables  that I used in my analysis instead of for all..<br></div><div>I got some errors for part5 which it runs without problem before. The data includes a sleep log file with some missingness for some samples. Would like to have a hint on this.<br></div><div><br></div><div>Thanks.<br></div><div>Best,<br></div><div>Dora<br></div></div><div><br></div><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div dir=\"ltr\">On Thu, Jun 4, 2020 at 2:38 PM Vincent van Hees &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">v.va...@movementdata.nl</a>&gt; wrote:<br></div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><div>Hi Dora, We do not have such an Excel file. If you were familiar with the old variable names I would hope that the new variable names are easy to match. If you can post your observed matching variable names here and the variable names you are uncertain then others may be able to help/learn from it.<br></div><div>Thanks,<br></div><div>Vincent <br></div><div><br></div><div>‐‐‐‐‐‐‐ Original Message ‐‐‐‐‐‐‐<br></div><div>On Monday 1 June 2020 17:45, Wei Guo &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">guowe...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:<br></div><div><br></div><blockquote type=\"cite\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Hi, Vincent,<br></div><div>Do you have a variable name  comparison between the old and new version? I have to update the variable names after I run ggir 2.0. Do you have some excel files for the variable definition since it takes more energy to figure out the variable names from the GGIR vignettes.<br></div><div>Thanks.<br></div><div>Best,<br></div><div><div>Dora<br></div><div><br></div><div>On Monday, May 4, 2020 at 4:59:09 PM UTC-4, Vincent van Hees wrote:<br></div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><div>GGIR version 2.0 is now on CRAN.<br></div><div><br></div><div>Main updates:<br></div><div>- <span style=\"background-color:rgb(245,248,250)\"><span style=\"color:rgb(20,23,26)\"><span style=\"font-family:system-ui,-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,&quot;Segoe UI&quot;,Roboto,Ubuntu,&quot;Helvetica Neue&quot;,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">GGIR now facilitates external function embedding, enabling you to apply your own R/Python functions to the raw data while benefiting from the existing data handling framework </span></span></span></span><a title=\"https://bit.ly/2YqBFJF\" href=\"https://t.co/1E0BBVNwyL?amp=1\" dir=\"ltr\" rel=\"nofollow\" style=\"background-color:rgb(245,248,250);color:rgb(27,149,224);font-style:normal;font-weight:400;font-stretch:inherit;font-size:15px;line-height:1.3125;font-family:system-ui,-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,&quot;Segoe UI&quot;,Roboto,Ubuntu,&quot;Helvetica Neue&quot;,sans-serif;list-style:none;margin:0px;text-align:start;text-decoration:none;border:0px solid black;display:inline;padding:0px;white-space:pre-wrap;min-width:0px;letter-spacing:normal;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;word-spacing:0px\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://t.co/1E0BBVNwyL?amp%3D1&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792490732676000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1eNp6EruEqWvgzLhgFykht\"><span style=\"color:inherit\"><span style=\"font-family:system-ui,-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,&quot;Segoe UI&quot;,Roboto,Ubuntu,&quot;Helvetica Neue&quot;,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:0px\">https://</span></span></span>bit.ly/2YqBFJF</a><span style=\"background-color:rgb(245,248,250)\"><span style=\"color:rgb(20,23,26)\"><span style=\"font-family:system-ui,-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFon